# Content is byte-stable across platforms.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows checks files out with CRLF while git stores LF.
# For source and config that is harmless. For content it is not, for two reasons:
#
#   1. `check:content` compares the committed artifact against a fresh generation
#      byte for byte. A CRLF checkout makes that comparison fail on every fresh
#      Windows clone, which turns a real drift gate into noise.
#   2. The generator embeds the markdown source inside the artifact and then into
#      D1. A CRLF checkout would put CRLF into the stored post body and into the
#      markdown served by /blog/:slug.md, so the same commit would produce
#      different content depending on which machine ran the generator.
#
# Pinning these paths to LF makes the generator's output depend on the content
# alone. Scoped deliberately: the rest of the tree keeps the default behaviour.

#   3. `build:diagrams` writes its SVG assets with LF and rewrites them in place
#      under `--force`. On a CRLF checkout every asset would come back as a diff
#      the moment anyone forced a rebuild, whether or not the drawing changed,
#      which is the churn the key is designed to avoid. The four `public/*.svg`
#      logo fixtures are deliberately NOT pinned: nothing regenerates them, and
#      `check:logo` is verified against a CRLF checkout.

#   4. `content/llms.txt` is the SOURCE of the `llms.txt` settings row and is
#      compared against that row byte for byte by `check:llms`. The live row is
#      LF, so a CRLF checkout would fail the gate on every fresh Windows clone
#      and, worse, would sync CRLF into D1 and change what /llms.txt serves. The
#      route inlines the same file, so a CRLF checkout would also have changed
#      the bytes served when the row is absent.

#   5. `CLAUDE.md` is measured by `check:claude-md`, which asserts it fits under
#      the context-window truncation limit and that the hard-rules pointer sits
#      near the top. Both are measurements of the FILE AS THE MACHINE HAS IT, so
#      without a pin the gate measures whichever line ending that machine
#      happens to carry. Found 2026-08-07, one commit after the gate shipped: the
#      author's working copy was LF and a fresh clone was CRLF, so the heading
#      needle `\n## Hard rules\n` matched on disk and not in a clean extraction.
#      The gate was green locally and RED on every fresh Windows clone. The
#      character count moves too, 8,479 against 8,610, which is harmless at this
#      size and would not be near the limit.
#      The gate was ALSO made line-ending agnostic in the same commit. Both, not
#      either: this pin fixes this file, and the normalization fixes the next
#      unpinned file somebody measures.

content/posts/*.md      text eol=lf
content/generated/*.json text eol=lf
content/llms.txt        text eol=lf
public/diagrams/*.svg   text eol=lf
CLAUDE.md               text eol=lf
